# HiREady SPA — configuration template
#
#   cp env.example .env
#
# `.env` is gitignored; this file is not, and must never contain a real secret.
#
# EVERYTHING HERE SHIPS TO THE BROWSER. Vite inlines every VITE_-prefixed
# variable into the public JavaScript bundle at build time, so anyone can read
# it with devtools. Nothing that needs protecting may ever be added to this
# file.
#
# Older setup docs in this repo told contributors to put VITE_GROQ_API_KEY and
# VITE_DEEPGRAM_API_KEY here. Those docs are gone. Both providers are called
# server-side; the browser gets a 60-second scoped Deepgram token and never
# sees a Groq key.

# ─── Required for a real deployment. ────────────────────────────────────────

# Base URL of the API, including /api.
#
# It falls back to http://localhost:5000/api when unset, which is convenient
# in development and dangerous in a build: a production bundle with this unset
# silently ships pointing at the user's own machine. Always set it explicitly
# when building.
#
#   local:   http://localhost:5000/api
#   docker:  /api            (nginx proxies to the backend, same origin)
#   deployed: https://api.hiready.example.com/api
VITE_API_URL=http://localhost:5000/api

# ─── Needed only for Google sign-in. ────────────────────────────────────────
#
# These are public identifiers by design — Firebase expects them in client
# code, and they are not credentials. What actually protects a Firebase
# project is its security rules and the authorised-domains list, so set both.
#
# Leave them blank to run with email/password sign-in only.

VITE_FIREBASE_API_KEY=
VITE_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=
VITE_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=
VITE_FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET=
VITE_FIREBASE_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID=
VITE_FIREBASE_APP_ID=
